<section id="my-philosophy" style="background:var(--cu-bone);position:relative;overflow:hidden;">
  <div class="paws faint"></div>

  <div class="container-wide" style="position:relative;z-index:1;">
    <div style="max-width:760px;margin:0 auto;">
      <!-- Heading -->
      <div style="margin-bottom:48px;text-align:center;">
        <div class="eyebrow" style="justify-content:center;">
          <span class="tick"></span>My philosophy · How I work and why
        </div>
        <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          The dog comes first. <span style="color:var(--cu-gold);">Then the work starts.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>

      <!-- Long-form body -->
      <div style="font:500 18px/1.75 var(--font-sans);color:var(--fg2);">

        <p style="margin:0 0 26px;">
          My job is the dog. That sounds obvious, but it's the first thing
          trainers lose sight of when a case gets hard or a client gets
          impatient. The dog comes first. Always. After that, my job is
          figuring out what actually works for you, because you're the one
          living with this animal for the next decade or so.
        </p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 26px;">
          Dogs are family. Not pets, not animals you take care of. Family.
          They give you everything they have, every day, no questions asked.
          The trade is that you show up for them. Their food, their
          movement, their rest, their relationships, all of it. You have
          to show up.
        </p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 26px;">
          That's the lens I bring to every dog I meet. I don't see your
          dog as broken. I'm not here to "fix" their personality. What I'm
          doing is figuring out why they're doing what they're doing, and
          what we need to change about the routine, the environment, or
          the way you're handling them so we get a different result.
        </p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 26px;">
          Every dog comes in with their own setup. Their personality,
          their breed traits, whatever they learned the hard way before
          they got to me, whatever genetics handed them. I take all of
          that seriously before I open my mouth about what to do next.
          Patience matters more than pressure. Clarity matters more than
          confusion. Slow progress beats fake progress.
        </p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 26px;">
          What I'm really teaching is your relationship with the dog.
          Obedience is a side effect of doing this right, not the goal.
          Your dog isn't a vending machine that spits out a "sit" when
          you press the right button. They're paying attention all day.
          To what's safe and what isn't, what gets them what they want,
          who's leading and who isn't, what they can count on from you
          and what they can't.
        </p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 26px;">
          I have one job in the room nobody else can do, which is speak
          up for the dog. They can't. So when something isn't working
          for them, even if it's working for you, I'll say so. That's
          part of what you're paying me for. The other part is teaching
          you what to do when I'm not there, because I won't be there
          for most of your dog's life.
        </p>

        <!-- Pull quote -->
        <blockquote style="margin:44px 0 44px;padding:32px 36px;background:#fff;border-radius:24px;border-left:6px solid var(--cu-gold);box-shadow:var(--shadow-sm);">
          <div style="font:600 22px/1.45 var(--font-display);color:var(--cu-navy);letter-spacing:-0.01em;">
            The reason you're hiring me isn't really to train your dog.
            It's so you can keep training them yourself, every day, for
            years after I'm gone.
          </div>
        </blockquote>

        <p style="margin:0 0 26px;">
          What does an easy, happy dog look like behind the scenes?
          Real food. Daily walks where they actually get to walk and
          sniff, not just heel for fifteen minutes. Time to play hard,
          fetch or tug or something that lets them be a dog for a while.
          A quiet spot to sleep without being bothered. And time with
          you that isn't about a command. Petting them. Hanging out.
          Being in the same room.
        </p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 26px;">
          That setup at home is not my job. It's yours. What I do is
          teach you the parts that need teaching.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin:48px 0 20px;font:900 24px var(--font-display);color:var(--cu-navy);letter-spacing:-0.01em;text-transform:uppercase;">
          What you're actually signing up for
        </h3>

        <p style="margin:0 0 26px;">
          When you book with me, you're signing up for a process with a
          beginning, a middle, and an end. I work under LIMA principles,
          which stands for Least Intrusive, Minimally Aversive. I've been
          applying all four quadrants of operant conditioning for more
          than ten years. If that's jargon to you, all it really means
          is I have more than one tool in the box, and I think hard about
          which one fits your dog and the situation we're working with.
        </p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 26px;">
          I don't do treat-only training. I don't do harsh corrections
          either. I do whatever the dog in front of me actually needs,
          given who they are, what they've learned, and what we're
          trying to change. That takes context, and there's no shortcut
          for it.
        </p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 26px;">
          What you end up with is a dog who understands what you're
          asking, and an owner who understands what their dog is saying
          back. That's what reliable behavior actually looks like. Not
          a trick that works at a training facility and falls apart the
          second you get home.
        </p>

        <p style="margin:0;font:700 18px/1.65 var(--font-sans);color:var(--cu-navy);padding:24px 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--border-strong);">
          Real change takes time. There's no version of this where I
          show up once and your dog is fixed forever. Anyone selling
          you that isn't a trainer.
        </p>

      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
